<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National University of San Marcos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1551">1551</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Universidad Nacional Mayor de San Marcos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National University of San Marcos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, was founded by a royal decree of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> on 12 May 1551 and is regarded as the oldest officially established university in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Run at first by the Dominicans, it trained clergy, lawyers and officials of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>, and it remains one of Peru's leading public universities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-university-of-san-marcos.jpg" alt="National University of San Marcos" width="320" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Universidad Nacional Mayor de San Marcos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UNMSM_coatofarms_seal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The university began in the general studies offered in the convent of the Rosario of the Order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, now the Basilica and Convent of Santo Domingo, on 1 July 1548. The cabildo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> sent Fray Tomás de San Martín and Captain Juan Jerónimo de Aliaga to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where they obtained the founding order from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles I of Spain</a>, who was Charles V of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and Queen Juana I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. The official foundation is dated 12 May 1551, and the university began to function officially on 2 January 1553. In 1571 it received the pontifical degree from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a> and became the Royal and Pontifical University of the City of the Kings of Lima.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The university was first governed by clerics of monastic orders, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon Reforms</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Age of Enlightenment</a> turned it into a secular institution. Between 1792 and 1811 the amphitheatre and the medicine chairs developed in the Royal Hospital of San Andrés. San Marcos, the College of Law and Letters of San Carlos and the College of Medicine of San Fernando were watched by the viceroy, since professors and students were suspected of subversion.</p><p>At the start of the 20th century student activists pressed for reform, and in 1909 students joined protests against Peruvian dictatorial governments. In 1916 the Federation of Students of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> was founded, led mainly by students from San Marcos, and it demanded updated curricula, the removal of untrained faculty and an end to government interference. The university system was later reorganised and autonomy was granted. Its first coat of arms, approved by Charles I in 1551, showed the Virgen del Rosario; after Pius V's bull of 1570 it gave way to the apostle Saint Mark and the lion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>San Marcos has been in several places. The first was the Dominican convent, the second near San Marcelo, and from 1575 the Plaza del Estanque, where the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> now stands. Since 1966 its main campus has been the University City, which holds 17 of its 20 faculties and the archaeological complex of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaca-san-marcos">Huaca San Marcos</a>. The Casona de San Marcos, its main cultural centre, is one of the buildings of the historic centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1988. In 2019 its colonial fund and founding documents, 1551 to 1852, entered UNESCO's Memory of the World Register.</p><p>The Faculty of Medicine San Fernando derives from the Royal College of Medicine and Surgery of San Fernando, created in 1811. Students of San Marcos founded the University Sports Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> on 7 August 1924. Its graduates and professors have included twenty-one presidents of Peru, and members of the university are called sanmarquinos.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National University of San Marcos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its <b>University of San Marcos</b>, founded in 1551, is among the oldest universities in the Americas, and the first book printed in South America, a catechism in Spanish, Quechua and Aymara, appeared there in 1584 following the Third Council of Lima.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2019 a doctoral thesis written entirely in Quechua was defended at the <b>National University of San Marcos</b> in Lima, founded in 1551, a sign of the language’s growing recognition.</blockquote><a href="/language/quechua">Quechua · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
